					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/whispersinthedark" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1749532); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1749532&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610118860" alt="WhispersInTheDark" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1749532)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/whispersinthedark" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1749532); return false;" >WhispersInTheDark</a></span> <span class="odate time_1399508923 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 May 2014 00:28</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2027775">
									

<p>I liked the initial format screw, and the revelation at the end was terrifying. The problem is, they don't necessarily seem like they belong in the same article, and there's a whole lot of stuff in-between that was a chore to read through. I'm not entirely sure what's supposed to be going on, either. I mean, I'm clear on what happens at the end, but I have no idea what most of the technobabble is about.</p>
<p>Also, there are some serious plot holes with the narrative. For starters, there's a strict rule that the O5s are <em>never</em> supposed to come into contact with anomalous objects. If the method they're using to extend their own lifespans is anomalous, that makes all of them enormous hypocrites, especially since it doesn't really benefit the Foundation as a whole in any way. If the method is non-anomalous, there's no good reason they wouldn't have released it to the general public, or at least to the rest of the Foundation.</p>
<p>More importantly, I find it extremely unlikely that they'd have actually reinstate Roger as an O5 after resurrecting him through anomalous means. They should have contained him right away, or at the very least, given him a strong amnestic and released him from Foundation service (while keeping him under close surveillance). Putting him back in a position of power when he's effectively a revenant just seems like it's asking for trouble.</p>
<p>I think this article would work a lot better if you trimmed it down considerably, and kept a tight focus on the revelation about the true nature of death, since that's the really interesting/horrifying part. Sometimes, less is more.</p>

							</div>

								
					
