<blockquote>
<p>True, the headcanon bit.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No. It doesn't make sense from an objective perspective because we will never know what seemingly-benign artifacts secretly harbor negative mind-affecting effects. What if the Fountain of Youth &#8212; assuming you allow it to exist in your headcanon &#8212; slowly, over the course of a century, slowly turned its users into manic sociopaths with no concern for the survival of mankind? How can you assure that the people who control the most powerful organization in the world are, themselves, not compromised? There is absolutely no reason why the O5s should have trusted an individual enough to let them come back from the dead and have their job back.</p>
<p>I'm downvoting for this, and various other things that stick out:</p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;credential token&quot; should probably be something like &quot;security key&quot;, as in a RSA authentication keyfob. What else would you be implying?</li>
<li>Of the three listed sorting algorithms, only one &#8212; the stoogesort &#8212; is recursive. Of the three, only one &#8212; the bogosort &#8212; has an unbounded search time; both of the others <em>will</em> eventually complete, so why did we use those to begin with? Also, a quantum bogosort is actually an <em>improvement</em> over the bogosort. Why is he recommending using a <em>more</em> efficient algorithm when the point is for us to delay or prevent its completion? Hell, if we're able to modify a search API on the fly &#8212; something that is mind-bogglingly weird to me &#8212; why not just break the code so it <em>never</em> completes? None of this makes sense to me as a computer scientist.</li>
<li>Why a &quot;Behemoth-class&quot; amnestic? What's wrong with the letter-class scale?</li>
</ul>

