														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2027854">
									

<p>My problem isn't just that the Overseers are breaking one of their own rules, it's that they're breaking a rule that makes perfect sense and seems necessary for the Foundation's survival. The O5s control the Foundation, and the risk of them being corrupted by some anomalous force is too great. It doesn't make sense that they'd use anomalous objects to prolong their lifespan, because they don't know what side effects might result from the use of said objects. That's what makes the objects anomalous in the first place, they can't be fully understood. How do the Overseers know that the magic potion that keeps them from aging won't also cause them to become radioactive, or result in demonic possession, or implant a subtle memetic command in the back of their minds that wouldn't be noticed until it's triggered?</p>
<p>Also, I feel like the types of people who'd be willing to break such an important rule to extend their own lifespans are exactly the types of people who shouldn't be in positions of power in the first place. As Smapti pointed out, any Overseer who had an &quot;I am the state!&quot; mentality about his role in the Foundation is unfit for the job.</p>
<p>From a narrative perspective, I also think that making the Overseers into immortal superhumans is a bad idea, since it changes the nature of the Foundation from &quot;normal humans containing the supernatural&quot; to &quot;supernatural beings containing other supernatural beings&quot;.</p>
<p>Most importantly of all, I don't think that giving the Overseers life extension technology actually contributes anything to the article, especially since the horror of the article is based on the inevitability of death. Much like the format screw and the ridiculously long wall of technobabble, that particular plot point only serves to distract the reader's attention away from the meat of the article.</p>

							</div>

								
