<p>Unless you and the other staff are about to go and fact-check articles for strict adherence to the guides and unilaterally make changes accordingly, no, there is in fact <em>no canon</em>.</p>
<p>Absent that, what you have are a series of guides with generally accepted precepts, that while logical and adopted for most works, are not immutable laws of fiction on the wiki. You and everyone else are free to downvote for non-accordance with those guides, but what you're referring to is a set of strongly-suggested recommendations. That you happened to be involved in putting them together and proumulgating them, and thus happen to feel exceptionally strongly about them, is utterly meaningless.</p>
<p>There are quite a few articles on the mainlist that violate both standard formatting and majority-held opinions on what the Foundation is. That everyone is suddenly shitting themselves over this one is puzzling to me, but that also doesn't matter. Then as now, the ultimate arbiter is the rating module. <em>Vox populi, vox Dei</em>, and people can just deal with it.</p>

