<blockquote>
<p>I would gladly do so, were I given the necessary permission.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Good luck with that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The voice of the people might be a strong factor in what works and what doesn't, but it's not the only factor and without going into the months-long argument about it, isn't even necessarily the most important one.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>According to you, sure. According to me too, to be honest, since there are articles on the mainlist with high ratings that I absolutely loathe. But what you or I personally like to see counts for nothing other than our votes and whatever minds we change with our commentary (I suspect that the influence of the latter is rather small). The most immediate and most powerful measure of quality is article rating. That's the closest we have to objective evaluation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We have guides that were put together with a lot of effort, and I honestly believe that sticking to the canon established there makes for a richer experience.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, and I'm not trying to be a jerk here, what you or I think to be essential to &quot;a richer experience&quot; doesn't matter. At all. The guides are a way to help a writer adjust the odds of success in their favor, but your presentation of them as tablets handed down from Mount Sinai is completely off the mark.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>but you cannot simply handwave all of that material away like it doesn't exist.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sure I can. I and many other authors have done exactly that and have had successful articles. As this article is indeed doing <em>right now</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Just because everything is a mess right now, doesn't mean we can't work to improve consistency.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Consistency is not the only thing we should be striving for. God forbid. There's a continuum of its importance, and in cases where consistency can be bent for the sake of a more interesting article on the mainlist, then it should give way. The point at which consistency is bent and not broken is something that's decided by the readership. I'm comfortable letting the members make that decision.</p>

